On Wed, 6 Dec 2006, Jim Miller wrote:
> On Wed, 06 Dec 2006 00:07:33 +0100, Patric Mueller <bhaak@bigfoot.com>
> wrote:
>>
>> I don't see a lot of duplicated effort in the development of Nethack
>> patches. Where do you see this effort?
>
> Look at some of the patch collection sites and note the overlap of
> work and concepts.  If you read enough of r.g.r.n, you'll fairly often
> see people announcing that they've made some enhancement, and someone
> else will say they already did a similar thing X years ago.

   Yeah, well, in practice that's okay. A lot of people just patch the
code for fun, or to practice their C and large-project skills; in those
cases it obviously doesn't matter who's done the same thing before. In
a lot of other cases, the new patch is /better/ than the old patch, so
it's a good thing the writer didn't get discouraged. Finally, most people
post YANIs in an undeveloped English form first, and let everyone discuss
them, and if there's already a similar patch, they're pointed to it at
that point. If after that they still want to go off and make their own
patch, more power to 'em.

> The lack of central coordination and a central repository results in
> many coding conflicts and abandoned projects.  This is magnified at
> the time of each official nethack release, when independent patches
> may be superseded, or have to be tediously reworked and reintegrated
> into the game, often over much time.
>
> This isn't an ecology.

   Um, no. It's also not an orangutan or a big pizza pie. It's NetHack,
a massive ancient computer program that people run for fun.

[...]
> Of course not everything would be accepted.  That's not what I'm
> saying.  But overall, more new ideas would be integrated.  There would
> be a much higher degree of organization and communication.  Work would
> be less duplicated and accomplished more quickly.  Releases wouldn't
> be glacial.

   Have you considered that maybe some people /like/ playing the same
game, or a substantially similar game, for years on end?  NetHack is
not Microsoft Office. We have no need for "work" to be "accomplished";
that's not even a meaningful metaphor with NetHack, because it's already
/in/ a working state. (Modulo the known-bugs list, of course, but it
doesn't sound like you're talking about bugfix releases here.)


Patric wrote:
>> I doubt that. When no one wants to do it, why should that be easier
>> with central, coordinated (who would coordinate) development?
>> Especially with a huge undertaking like OO-nethack. You can't do that
>> in small incremental steps.

   FWIW, I think a "professional software-development" model would help
for something like OO-nethack. However, I don't think OO-nethack is a
worthwhile goal, so that's not a reason for me to support a "professional"
model. (Note that the DevTeam is /already/ a "team"; it's right there in
their name.)

> Teamwork accomplishes amazing things.  The lone wolf programmer model
> isn't delivering here.

   Which do you mean --- the "lone wolf" DevTeam isn't releasing new
versions, or the "lone wolf" Usenet community isn't releasing patches?
(There's also the mass of "lone wolves" out there in the Real World,
about whom we know very little because they don't post to r.g.r.n.)


   In short: The only thing I'd like to see different about the DevTeam's
current approach is I'd like to see an official bugfix patch database
on www.nethack.org. Each "fixed" bug should have a link to the patch
that fixes it. Then, anyone who "just can't wait" could fix the bugs
they care about, quickly, without touching the rest of the game. (And
for the barbarians, a "most recent bugfixed version" could be available
in binary, too; but that would IMO be an unfair drain on the DevTeam
if it were updated too often, and an unfair tease to the players if it
were updated too rarely.)

